  "ch2.read.b2": "2-year price change",
  "ch2.read.b10": "10-year price change",
  "ch2.read.b30": "30-year price change",
  "ch2.unit.pp": "{v} percentage points",
  "ch2.unit.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch2.unit.durpair": "{a}% / {b}%",
  "ch2.read.durL": "{T}-year: duration approximation / with convexity",
  "ch2.cv.x": "Change in yield (percentage points) →",
  "ch2.cv.y": "↑ Price change (%)",
  "ch2.cv.2022": "2022",
  "ch2.say.up": "Yield rises {dy} percentage points: the 2-year falls {p2}%, the 30-year falls <em>{p30}%</em>, {ratio} times as much as the former. For the {T}-year, the duration approximation gives {dur}%, with convexity {dc}%, and the exact value is {ex}%.",
  "ch2.say.down": "Yield falls {dy} percentage points: the 2-year rises {p2}%, the 30-year rises <em>{p30}%</em>. For the {T}-year, the duration approximation gives +{dur}%, less than the exact +{ex}%: convexity makes the falls smaller and the rises larger.",
  "ch2.say.zero": "With no change in yield, the price does not change. Drag Δy.",
  "ch2.say.y2022": "2022: the 2-year yield rose from 0.73% to 4.41%, the 10-year from 1.52% to 3.88%, and the 30-year from 1.90% to 3.97%. Suppose the three bonds were issued at par at the end of 2021 at the yields prevailing then, and repriced at the end-2022 yields: they fall {p2}%, {p10}% and <em>{p30}%</em> respectively (not counting the interest received during the year, nor the one year shorter remaining maturity).",
  "ch2.p3": "This is what happened in 2022. The Fed's first rate hike took effect on 17 March 2022, and yields rose sharply across all maturities. The US Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) estimated that by the end of 2022, US banks held securities with about $620 billion of unrealised losses. Silicon Valley Bank had put large amounts of its deposits into long-dated securities. The Fed's review of April 2023 wrote that at the end of 2022 its held-to-maturity securities portfolio had a weighted-average duration of 6.2 years, and about 94% of its deposits were above the deposit-insurance limit. By duration arithmetic, a one-point rise in yield would cut the market value of such a portfolio by about 6%. On the books, these losses could be left out of profit; but once depositors ran and the bank was forced to sell, the losses became real. On 10 March 2023 the regulators closed the bank. Why a bank that made no bad loans can still fail is explained by a bank-run model in chapter 3 of the site essay <a href=\"../where-money-comes-from/#ch3\">Where Money Comes From</a>.",
  "ch2.p4": "Duration is not unique to bonds. Any “stream of future money” has a duration, and so the same sensitivity to interest rates: stocks and mortgages (chapter 8), and the government's interest bill (chapter 11).",
  "ch2.see": "For the intuitive version of why old bonds must fall when rates rise, see “Interest Rates & Bonds,” <a href=\"../../../investment/rates/interest-rates-bonds/#step4\">step 4</a>.",
  "ch2.limit": "Model limits: no default, annual coupons, and the yields of the three maturities move in parallel. In reality, different maturities move by different amounts (in 2022 short yields rose the most), which is the subject of the next chapter.",

  /* ───── 03 ───── */
  "ch3.title": "The Yield Curve: One Curve That Holds a Quote for Every Future Year",
  "ch3.law": "Join the yields across maturities into a curve, then peel it apart layer by layer, and you can read off the one-year rates for future years that the market will agree to today. These are called forward rates.",
  "ch3.p1": "The <strong>yield curve</strong> plots the yields of the same borrower (usually the government) across different maturities on one chart. The US Treasury publishes a set of “par yields” every day: for a hypothetical Treasury bond issued at face value, the coupon it would have to carry for its price to be exactly 100.",
  "ch3.p2": "Par yields are an average. The ten coupons of a 10-year bond fall in ten different years, and each should be discounted at its own rate. Starting from the 1-year and solving upward one year at a time gives each year's own discount factor; this method is called <strong>bootstrapping</strong>. From the discount factors one can compute the <strong>spot rate</strong> (the zero-coupon rate: the yield on a bond that pays a single sum in year <i>n</i>) and the <strong>forward rate</strong> (the borrowing rate for “year <i>n</i>−1 to year <i>n</i>” that can be locked in today).",
  "ch3.eq": "1 = <i>c<sub>n</sub></i>·(<i>DF</i><sub>1</sub> + … + <i>DF</i><sub><i>n</i>−1</sub>) + (1 + <i>c<sub>n</sub></i>)·<i>DF<sub>n</sub></i><br><i>z<sub>n</sub></i> = <i>DF<sub>n</sub></i><sup>−1/<i>n</i></sup> − 1  <i>f</i><sub><i>n</i>−1→<i>n</i></sub> = <i>DF</i><sub><i>n</i>−1</sub>/<i>DF<sub>n</sub></i> − 1",
  "dg.fwd.aria": "Diagram: a spot rate runs from today to the end of year n, while a forward rate covers just one year of it; the numbers come from the US Treasury curve of 30 June 2023",
  "dg.fwd.head": "One curve, two readings (US, 30 June 2023)",
  "dg.fwd.spot": "{n}-year spot {v}%",
  "dg.fwd.fwd": "Year {n}: {v}%",
  "dg.fwd.today": "Today",
  "dg.fwd.year": "End yr {n}",
  "dg.fwd.legSpot": "Cyan: the spot rate, the annual rate for borrowing from today to the end of year n.",
  "dg.fwd.legFwd": "Gold: the forward rates, fixed today, each for one year. Compounded over n years they equal the n-year spot rate.",
  "dg.fwd.cap": "Figure 3. The relation between spot and forward rates (schematic: the height of the lines does not show the rate level; the numbers come from bootstrapping the day's par yields with the model below). The long cyan line is made of short gold arrows joined end to end: the 3-year spot is below the 1-year because the year-2 and year-3 forward rates are lower.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Bootstrapping spot and forward rates from US Treasury par yields",
  "ch3.model.how": "The white dots are the par yields published by the US Treasury (1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 10, 20 and 30-year, daily data). Pick a date and look at the cyan spot curve and the gold forward curve; then drag “maturity <i>n</i>” to read off that year's par yield, spot rate, forward rate and discount factor.",
