  "ch3.unit.pct": "{v}%",
  "ch3.unit.years": "{v} years",
  "ch3.cv.x": "Maturity (years) →",
  "ch3.cv.y": "↑ Rate (%)",
  "ch3.say.one": "There are no earlier years to peel off before year 1, so the three rates are the same: {z}%. Drag to the right.",
  "ch3.say.inverted": "On this day the curve is <em>inverted</em>: the {n}-year spot rate is {z}%, below the 1-year's {z1}%. The forward rate from year {n1} to year {n} is only <em>{f}%</em>: the “future rates” the market agrees to today are lower than the current rate.",
  "ch3.say.normal": "On this day the curve slopes upward: the {n}-year spot rate is {z}%, above the 1-year's {z1}%. The forward rate from year {n1} to year {n} is <em>{f}%</em>.",
  "ch3.p3": "At the end of June 2023 the curve was inverted: the 1-year was 5.40%, the 10-year spot rate 3.76%, and the forward rate from year 9 to year 10 only 3.24%. At the end of 2021 it was the opposite: the 1-year at 0.39%, and the forward rate from year 9 to year 10 at 1.78%. Forward rates swing more than spot rates because a forward rate is the “marginal” year, while a spot rate is the average over all the years before it. The next chapter asks: why is the forward rate this number?",
  "ch3.limit": "Model limits: par yields are joined by straight-line interpolation, with annual coupons. The Treasury's own curve uses semi-annual coupons and a smoothing spline, so the spot rates here can differ from the official zero-coupon curve by a few basis points. Straight-line interpolation also makes forward rates jump at the nodes (for example around year 20); that comes from the interpolation method, not from information in the market.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "Why the Curve Has This Shape: Expectations, Plus a Waiting Fee",
  "ch4.law": "The long-term rate is roughly the average of the future short-term rates the market expects, plus a term premium that compensates for the risk of locking money up for longer. When the curve inverts, it usually means the market expects rate cuts ahead.",
  "ch4.p1": "You can buy a 10-year government bond, or buy a 1-year bond and roll it over every year for ten years. If investors cared only about the average return, the expected returns of the two strategies should be the same, so the 10-year rate equals the average of expected short-term rates over the next ten years. This is called the <strong>expectations hypothesis</strong>. But locking money up for ten years carries the risk of rate changes and inflation surprises along the way, so investors usually demand a little more for doing it. That compensation is called the <strong>term premium</strong>.",
  "ch4.eq": "<i>y<sub>n</sub></i> ≈ (1/<i>n</i>)·∫<sub>0</sub><sup><i>n</i></sup> E[<i>i<sub>t</sub></i>] d<i>t</i> + <i>TP<sub>n</sub></i>  E[<i>i<sub>t</sub></i>] = <i>i<sub>L</sub></i> + (<i>i</i><sub>0</sub> − <i>i<sub>L</sub></i>)·2<sup>−<i>t</i>/<i>h</i></sup>",
  "ch4.p2": "This uses the simplest possible expected path: the short rate moves from today's <i>i</i><sub>0</sub> toward a long-run level <i>i<sub>L</sub></i>, closing half the gap every <i>h</i> years. The term premium rises in step with maturity, reaching <i>TP</i><sub>10</sub> at 10 years and no higher after that. The shape of the path, the number of years for the gap to halve, and the shape of the term premium are assumptions made for illustration, not estimates; a real term premium can only be estimated with a model, as shown below.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Expectations + term premium = the yield curve",
  "ch4.model.how": "The grey dashed line is the expected short-rate path you set; the cyan line is the yield from expectations alone; the gold line is the yield after adding the term premium. The default is a short rate of 4% and a long-run level of 3%: the market expects rate cuts. Lower the long-run level further to see when the curve inverts; then raise the term premium to see it bend the curve back.",
  "ch4.model.aria": "The expected short-rate path, the yield curve from expectations alone, and the yield curve after adding the term premium",
  "ch4.leg.path": "Expected short rate",
