  "ch4.say.inverted": "The 10-year minus 3-month spread is <em>{sp}</em> percentage points and the curve is inverted: the market expects the short rate to fall from {i0}% toward {iL}%, which pushes the long rate below the short rate. The term premium is {tp} percentage points.",
  "ch4.say.invtp": "The spread is <em>{sp}</em> percentage points and the curve is inverted, but this time it is not because of expected rate cuts: the term premium is negative ({tp} percentage points), meaning investors are willing to accept a lower return to hold long-term bonds.",
  "ch4.say.flat": "The spread is only {sp} percentage points and the curve is nearly flat. Of the 10-year yield, the expectations part is {e}% and the term premium {tp} percentage points, which together come to about the 3-month rate.",
  "ch4.say.normal": "The spread is <em>{sp}</em> percentage points and the curve slopes up. Of the 10-year yield, the expectations part is {e}% and the term premium {tp} percentage points.",
  "ch4.p3": "The term premium cannot be seen; it has to be estimated from data with a model. Two commonly used estimates are the Kim–Wright model of the Federal Reserve Board and the ACM model of the New York Fed, which sometimes differ by several dozen basis points. The purple line in the chart below is the 10-year term premium estimated by Kim–Wright (monthly averages): it peaked at about 2.45 percentage points in the early 1990s, fell to about −0.61 around 2020, and was about 0.9 in early November 2026. Next comes the best-known use of this curve: inversion and recession.",
  "ch4.hist.title": "The US 10-year minus 3-month spread, and past recessions",
  "ch4.hist.how": "The cyan line is the 10-year Treasury yield minus the 3-month Treasury bill yield (monthly averages, with the bill converted to a bond-equivalent yield); the red shading marks recessions dated by the National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER). Drag “lead time”; call it N months. <strong>Called</strong>: the spread was negative in at least one of the N months before the recession began. <strong>False alarm</strong>: an inversion episode with no recession starting between its first month and N months after its last month. Inversions less than 6 months apart count as one episode. Green dots above the chart mark recessions that were called and red circles mark misses; gold triangles below the chart mark the month each false-alarm inversion began. The sentence under the chart also gives a stricter count, in which the N months run from the first inverted month.",
